    {
      "meaning": "Jubilee Year and Freedom",
      "description": "The number fifty represents freedom, release, and restoration. The fiftieth year was the Jubilee year, when slaves were freed, debts were canceled, and land was returned to its original owners. Pentecost (Shavuot) is celebrated on the fiftieth day after Pesach, and the Holy Spirit was poured out on Pentecost.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is good and theologically correct, but lacks some interesting additional facts that could enrich understanding. Specifically, the connection between the Jubilee year in Leviticus 25 and Pentecost in Acts 2 could be expanded with footnotes. Also relevant are the Hebrew word 'yovel' (Jubilee) and the rabbinic tradition that the Torah was given at Sinai on the 50th day after the Exodus from Egypt.; [minor] It would have been useful to add footnotes providing in-depth information without disrupting the flow of the main text, for example regarding the Hebrew term and the rabbinic tradition about the giving of the Torah.",
      "score": 7,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "The Hebrew word for Jubilee, 'yovel' (יובל), originally means 'ram's horn' or 'billy goat,' referring to the fact that the Jubilee year was announced by blowing the shofar on Yom Kippur (the Day of Atonement).",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "According to rabbinic tradition, the Torah was given at Mount Sinai exactly fifty days after the Exodus from Egypt, forming the basis for Shavuot as the festival of the giving of the Torah.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In Christian liturgical tradition, Pentecost (Greek: pentekostē, 'the fiftieth') has been understood as a fulfillment of the promise of the Jubilee year—where physical liberation in ancient Israel points forward to the Spirit's liberation in Christ.",
      "source": "liturgisk"
    }
  ]
}
